import re

p3_prose = """<p>Endel Tulving formalized a foundational neurocognitive taxonomy distinguishing two independent subtypes of declarative (explicit) memory: episodic memory and semantic memory. Episodic memory supports the conscious recollection of personally experienced events situated in unique subjective spatiotemporal contexts ("mental time travel"). Tulving termed this experiential state autonoetic consciousness ("self-knowing"), which permits individuals to re-experience past events and project hypothetical scenarios into personal future time. In contrast, semantic memory encompasses generalized, decontextualized factual knowledge, linguistic definitions, and conceptual schemas. Semantic retrieval operates under noetic consciousness ("knowing"), lacking personal chronological or spatial tags.</p>
<p>Neuropsychological lesions demonstrate a striking double dissociation between these two explicit systems. Bilateral medial temporal lobe (MTL) damage—specifically targeting the hippocampus and parahippocampal gyrus—severely disrupts episodic encoding and retrieval while preserving preexisting semantic knowledge and the acquisition of decontextualized facts. Conversely, semantic dementia (SD)—a progressive neurodegenerative variant of frontotemporal lobar degeneration characterized by anterior temporal lobe (ATL) atrophy—degrades conceptual meaning and naming while sparing recent autobiographical episodic recollection.</p>
<p>To characterize this dissociation, cognitive neuroscientists evaluated 45 age-matched participants across three cohorts: Healthy Controls, Medial Temporal Lobe Amnesia patients (MTL), and Semantic Dementia patients (SD). All participants completed three assays: (1) an Autobiographical Memory Interview quantifying internal episodic details (specific events, perceptual descriptions, emotional states) versus external details (generalized facts, historical semantic commentary); (2) the Pyramids and Palm Trees Test (PPTT) assessing non-verbal semantic associative knowledge; and (3) an Episodic Future Thinking paradigm requiring participants to construct plausible, novel personal scenarios set five years in the future. Behavioral metrics and normalized regional gray matter volumes are summarized in Table 1.</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p3_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
